.TH ACTIVITY-RELAY-DIRECTORY 1 "13 August 2026" "Activity-Relay Directory" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
activity-relay-directory \- signed relay directory service for Activity-Relay
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B activity-relay-directory
.br
.B activity-relay-directory --version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B activity-relay-directory
runs the Activity-Relay Directory HTTP service and its local SQLite-backed
state. Runtime configuration is supplied through environment variables.
.PP
The Debian package installs the service disabled and does not start it
automatically. Its default environment binds only to loopback and keeps
lifecycle handling, public listing, automatic soft pruning, positive inactive
retention, and administrator email disabled.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --version
Print the embedded application version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The packaged systemd service reads
.BR /etc/default/activity-relay-directory .
Optional public operator metadata is read from
.BR /etc/activity-relay-directory/config.yml
when that file is present, or from the clean absolute path selected by
.BR DIRECTORY_CONFIG_PATH .
Review configuration before enabling or starting the service.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/default/activity-relay-directory
Package-managed runtime environment.
.TP
.B /etc/activity-relay-directory/config.yml
Optional operator-owned public contact metadata; absent by default.
.TP
.B /usr/share/doc/activity-relay-directory/examples/config.yml.example
Packaged operator-metadata example.
.TP
.B /var/lib/activity-relay-directory/directory.sqlite
Default SQLite database path. The state directory is intentionally preserved
when the package is removed or purged.
.SH SECURITY
Do not enable lifecycle handling or public exposure merely because the package
is installed. Review the trusted-proxy, HTTPS public-base, retention, database
growth, and mail settings documented with the package before activation.
.SH SEE ALSO
The packaged README and documentation under
.B /usr/share/doc/activity-relay-directory/
cover protocol, persistence, moderation, retention, reverse proxying, release,
and rollback behavior.
